Devin, following the call with Simone, here is the one methodology point I wo=
uld put in front of whoever writes your paper.

We code non-disclosure as unknown, never as absence. If forty transactions di=
sclose a term and thirty say nothing, we report the frequency across the fort=
y and we report the count. What we do not do is treat the thirty as evidence =
that the term was absent.

The practical consequence is that a frequency from our data is a frequency am=
ong disclosing transactions. If your paper describes it as a frequency among =
all transactions, the sentence is wrong even though the number is right.

I mention it because that specific error appears in about a third of the clie=
nt documents I am shown, and it is usually introduced by whoever summarizes r=
ather than by whoever read the source.

